Object.assign(window.ACADEMY_DATA.exams, {
"latin": [
{"q":"Mark the stressed syllable in 'amare' (to love):","choices":["A-ma-re","a-MA-re","a-ma-RE","a-ma-re (no stress)"],"answer":1,"explain":"The penult 'ma' is long by nature (ama-re: the a is long), so it takes the stress: a-MA-re. The last syllable is never stressed."},
{"q":"What is the accusative singular of 'servus'?","choices":["servus","servi","servo","servum"],"answer":3,"explain":"Second-declension masculine accusative singular is stem + -um: servum. Servus is nominative, servi is genitive singular or nominative plural, servo is dative/ablative."},
{"q":"Translate: 'Non scholae sed vitae discimus.'","choices":["We learn not for school but for life.","We do not teach in schools for life.","The school of life teaches nothing.","Not scholars but lives teach us."],"answer":0,"explain":"Seneca's famous line. Scholae and vitae are dative ('for school,' 'for life'), non...sed = 'not...but,' discimus = 'we learn.' The motto of every honest education."},
{"q":"Choose the correct form: 'The girls are good.'","choices":["Puellae sunt bonae.","Puellae est bona.","Puellae sunt boni.","Puella bona sunt."],"answer":0,"explain":"Puellae is nominative plural feminine, so the verb must be sunt (plural) and the adjective bonae (feminine plural). Est is singular, boni is masculine, and 'puella bona' is singular."},
{"q":"In 'cum amicis,' what case is 'amicis'?","choices":["Accusative","Ablative","Genitive","Dative"],"answer":1,"explain":"Cum always takes the ablative: cum amicis = 'with friends.' The -is ending here is ablative plural (2nd declension). Cum never takes the accusative."},
{"q":"'Caveat emptor' means:","choices":["Let the buyer beware.","The buyer has paid.","Empty promises deceive.","Seize the bargain."],"answer":0,"explain":"Caveat = 'let him/her beware' (subjunctive of cavere), emptor = buyer. The legal principle that the buyer is responsible for checking quality — still cited in contract law."},
{"q":"Which ending marks the third person plural present?","choices":["-mus","-tis","-nt","-s"],"answer":2,"explain":"-nt = 'they': amant (they love), laborant (they work), sunt is the exception (irregular esse). -mus = we, -tis = you (plural), -s = you (singular)."},
{"q":"Translate: 'Si vis pacem, para bellum.'","choices":["If you want peace, prepare for war.","Since war is peaceful, prepare.","If peace visits, avoid war.","War prepares those who want peace."],"answer":0,"explain":"Si = if, vis = you want, pacem = peace (accusative), para = prepare! (imperative), bellum = war (accusative). The Roman doctrine of deterrence in six words."},
{"q":"D.M. at the start of a Roman tombstone stands for:","choices":["Dis Manibus — to the spirits of the dead","Deo Maximo — to the greatest god","Domus Memoriae — house of memory","Dedit Monumentum — he gave this monument"],"answer":0,"explain":"D.M. = Dis Manibus, 'to the spirits of the dead' — the standard opening of Roman epitaphs, followed by the name and 'vixit annis...' (he/she lived N years)."},
{"q":"'In vino veritas' means:","choices":["In wine, truth.","Wine is the truth.","Truth in advertising.","The vineyard is true."],"answer":0,"explain":"In with the ablative = 'in'; vino = wine (ablative), veritas = truth (nominative). 'In wine there is truth' — people speak honestly when drinking. Veritas is also Harvard's motto."}
]
});
