Object.assign(window.ACADEMY_DATA.quizzes, {
"arithmetic:1": [
{"q":"Fermi asked 'how many piano tuners are in Chicago?' expecting no data at all. What is the point of such a question?","choices":["To train students to look up exact answers faster","To teach breaking an unknowable question into guessable pieces that multiply","To prove experts memorize more facts than students","To show that most real questions have no useful answer"],"answer":1,"explain":"Fermi problems train the method, not the answer: decompose the unknown into pieces you can roughly guess (households, pianos per household, tunings per year, tunings per tuner), multiply them, and land within a factor of two. It is how engineers, investors, and generals sanity-check claims before the spreadsheets arrive."},
{"q":"A million seconds is about 11 days; a billion seconds is about 32 years. A budget 'grows from a million to a billion — just a bit bigger.' What is the real scale of the change?","choices":["About 10 times bigger","About 100 times bigger","About 1,000 times bigger","About a million times bigger"],"answer":2,"explain":"A billion is a thousand million — three orders of magnitude, not 'a bit.' The seconds comparison makes the gap visceral: 11 days versus 32 years. Most people fooled by numbers are fooled by exactly this: they never converted to a scale their brain can feel, so 'million' and 'billion' blur into 'both big.'"},
{"q":"Your Fermi estimate of your town's daily gasoline use is 10,000 gallons/day; a friend's is 18,000. A news graphic claims 8,000,000 gallons/day. What should you conclude?","choices":["Both estimates are useless since they disagree","You and your friend are both in the right neighborhood; the news figure deserves suspicion","The news figure is most trustworthy because it is precise","Average all three numbers together"],"answer":1,"explain":"10,000 and 18,000 differ by less than a factor of two — the whole point of Fermi estimation is landing in the right neighborhood, which both of you did. The news figure is off by roughly a factor of 800, which signals a broken base or a trick. Precision without a sane method is the least trustworthy number of all: most bad decisions come from being wrong by hundreds, not by two."}
],
"arithmetic:2": [
{"q":"A jacket costs $80. It is marked 50% off, then the store adds 50% back 'for restocking.' What is the final price?","choices":["$80 — the changes cancel out","$70","$60","$40"],"answer":2,"explain":"Successive changes multiply, they do not add: $80 x 0.5 = $40, then $40 x 1.5 = $60. The round trip made the store $20 while intuition insisted the changes cancel. That asymmetry — exploited by merchants for centuries — is why you prove it with multiplication, not gut feeling."},
{"q":"A headline says 'unemployment fell 20%.' What must you know before reacting?","choices":["The base: 20% of what rate? Falling 10% to 8% is 20% — but so is 5% to 4%, a very different story","Nothing — 20% is always a large drop","That it fell by 20 percentage points","That one-fifth of workers found jobs"],"answer":0,"explain":"Percent of what? A percentage without its base is a story without a setting. You also need the absolute change (percentage points, not just percent) and the timeframe. 'Risk doubled!' means nothing until you hear: from 1 in a million to 2 in a million, or from 1 in 4 to 1 in 2?"},
{"q":"Two coffee bags: 12 oz for $4.80, or 20 oz for $7.00. Which is the better deal?","choices":["The 12 oz bag — the sticker price is lower","The 20 oz bag — $0.35/oz beats $0.40/oz","They are exactly equal deals","Impossible to tell without tasting them"],"answer":1,"explain":"Always divide price by quantity: $4.80/12 = $0.40 per ounce; $7.00/20 = $0.35 per ounce. The bigger bag wins. The 'big deal' package is often the expensive one — unit pricing is the two-second audit that defeats the shelf illusion."}
],
"arithmetic:3": [
{"q":"What is a 15% tip on a $47.60 bill, computed mentally?","choices":["$7.14","$6.19","$9.52","$7.60"],"answer":0,"explain":"Anchor on 10%: move the decimal one place — 10% of $47.60 is $4.76. Five percent is half of that: $2.38. Add: $4.76 + $2.38 = $7.14. The 10% trick builds 5%, 15%, and 20% from one instant anchor — no calculator, and every number you check in your head is one nobody can lie to you about."},
{"q":"Why compute 8 x 47 as (8 x 40) + (8 x 7) instead of grinding through it?","choices":["It is faster on paper","Working memory holds about 4 chunks; splitting makes two easy problems plus one addition","The Greeks required all multiplication to be split","Multiplication only works on round numbers"],"answer":1,"explain":"Working memory holds roughly four chunks, not long digit strings. Splitting turns one hard problem into 320 + 56 = 376 — each piece small enough to hold without dropping anything. The method fits the hardware of your head: that is why mental math is about restructuring, not about being a human calculator."},
{"q":"A $65 shirt is 20% off. What do you pay, mentally?","choices":["$52","$45","$55","$58.50"],"answer":0,"explain":"10% of $65 is $6.50, so 20% is double: $13. Then $65 - $13 = $52. Round-and-adjust plus the 10% anchor turns a register problem into ten seconds of head math. Speed comes later — accuracy first, and the estimate in your head is the audit on every receipt."}
],
"arithmetic:4": [
{"q":"In Euclid's proof, you assume finitely many primes, multiply them all, and add 1 to get N. Why does N destroy the assumption?","choices":["N is always itself a prime number","N is divisible by none of the listed primes, so its prime divisor must be new — contradicting the list's completeness","N is too large to have any prime divisor","Adding 1 makes N even, which breaks the list"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each listed prime divides the product, so each leaves remainder 1 when dividing N. But every whole number above 1 has some prime divisor — so N's prime divisor cannot be on the list. The list can never be complete: the primes never end. Note the subtlety students miss: N itself need not be prime; it only needs a new prime factor."},
{"q":"What makes Euclid's argument a proof rather than merely a very convincing argument?","choices":["It was written by Euclid","Its conclusion follows necessarily from its premises — no future evidence can overturn it","It uses impressively large numbers","It has been believed for 2,300 years"],"answer":1,"explain":"A proof leaves no loophole in principle: given the premises, the conclusion must follow. A convincing argument — even a scientific one — is always hostage to new data. That is why Euclid's proof still works today, unchanged after 2,300 years: it depends on no fact about the world, only on reasoning."},
{"q":"Prove that the sum of two odd numbers is even. Which working is correct?","choices":["(2k+1) + (2m+1) = 2(k+m+1), which has the form 2 x (whole number) — hence even","Odd + odd = odd, so the claim is false","3 + 5 = 8, and one example proves it","Odd numbers cannot be written as 2k+1"],"answer":0,"explain":"Write the odds as 2k+1 and 2m+1 for whole numbers k and m. Their sum is 2k + 2m + 2 = 2(k+m+1) — visibly twice a whole number, hence even, for every k and m. The power is generality: it settles all odd numbers at once, where the single example 3+5=8 settles only itself."}
],
"arithmetic:5": [
{"q":"Nine people earn $30k; one earns $3M. The town's mean income is $327k and its median is $30k. A job ad cites the $327k 'average.' What is the defense?","choices":["Ask which average — the median ($30k) describes the typical resident; the mean is dragged by one outlier","All averages are lies, so ignore the number entirely","The median is wrong because it ignores the millionaire","$327k is close enough to $30k"],"answer":0,"explain":"The mean is pulled by outliers: one $3M earner lifts the 'average' elevenfold, while the median — the middle value — resists outliers and tells the job-seeker what a typical resident earns. Whenever someone quotes an average about money, the first question is always: which average?"},
{"q":"'The test is 99% accurate.' A disease affects 1 person in 10,000. Why might most positive results still be false?","choices":["Because 99% is not a real number","The base rate dominates: 1% of 9,999 healthy people (~100 false positives) dwarfs the 1 true positive","Because accuracy and precision are the same thing","Because no test exceeds 90% accuracy"],"answer":1,"explain":"Test 10,000 people: the 1 sick person tests positive, but 1% of the 9,999 healthy — about 100 people — also test positive. So roughly 100 of 101 positives are false alarms. '99% accurate' without the base rate is a story without a setting; rare conditions make most positives false."},
{"q":"Headline: 'Crime up 40% since the new mayor took office.' What do you ask first?","choices":["40% of what? A rise from 5 to 7 incidents is 'up 40%' and means almost nothing","Which party the mayor belongs to","What the mayor's approval rating is","Whether crime will fall next year"],"answer":0,"explain":"Run the four master questions: compared to what, measured how, over which period, and who benefits from me believing it? '40% of what' is decisive — 5 to 7 versus 500 to 700. Then check the window (cherry-picked start dates manufacture trends) and whether timing is being confused with causation."}
],
"arithmetic:6": [
{"q":"Estimate how many words you have spoken in your life. Which decomposition is in the Fermi spirit?","choices":["~5,000 words/day x 365 days x your age in years — rough but independent pieces, multiplied","Count every word said yesterday and multiply by your exact age in days","Look it up — estimation is pointless","Ask a linguist for the official figure"],"answer":0,"explain":"Fermi's method: break the unknown into guessable pieces and multiply. ~5,000 spoken words a day is a plausible guess; times 365, times your age. For a 47-year-old: 5,000 x 365 x 47 is about 86 million words — within a factor of two of reality, which is the whole point. Rough pieces, right neighborhood."},
{"q":"In a stats teardown, a headline boasts 'markets up 60% since March!' Which element is the cherry-picked window?","choices":["The start date: chosen at a dip, it manufactures a boom — demand the longer series","The use of the median","The citation of a primary source","The inclusion of the base rate"],"answer":0,"explain":"Any trend can be manufactured by choosing the start date: begin the clock at a dip and any recovery looks like a boom. The teardown asks what window was chosen and what the longer series shows. A fair presentation shows the full context, not the flattering slice."},
{"q":"Which single habit defeats both bad Fermi problems and statistical manipulation?","choices":["Never trusting any number at all","Supplying the context yourself: decomposing claims into checkable pieces and asking compared-to-what, measured-how, over-which-period","Only reading peer-reviewed papers","Memorizing more formulas"],"answer":1,"explain":"Both skills are one habit: refuse numbers that arrive without context, and supply the context yourself. Fermi estimation builds the context from scratch; the four master questions interrogate someone else's. Together they make you the auditor of every figure you meet — permanently suspicious of context-free numbers, permanently able to supply the context."}
]
});

Object.assign(window.ACADEMY_DATA.quizzes, {
"geometry:1": [
{"q":"Why did Euclid bother writing down postulates for things everyone already believed?","choices":["To make the book longer and more impressive","Explicit assumptions make reasoning checkable — and let later thinkers question the foundations, as happened with the parallel postulate","The Greeks required exactly five of everything","Because the postulates were controversial at the time"],"answer":1,"explain":"Foundations on the table mean anyone can verify every step — and anyone can question the foundations themselves. Two thousand years of trying to prove the fifth postulate from the others produced non-Euclidean geometry, which turned out to describe curved spacetime. Disputes are usually about the fifth postulate: the assumption nobody examined."},
{"q":"Which postulate is the load-bearing odd one out, and why?","choices":["The parallel postulate: 'through a point not on a line, exactly one parallel line' — far less obvious than the other four","The first — drawing a line between two points","The third — drawing a circle with any center and radius","The fourth — all right angles are equal"],"answer":0,"explain":"The first four feel self-evident; the fifth makes a strong claim about infinity — that parallels never meet, no matter how far extended. Its non-obviousness is exactly why mathematicians spent two millennia trying to derive it, and why its failure on curved surfaces revolutionized physics. In any argument, notice which premises are load-bearing and which are merely assumed."},
{"q":"A debater keeps 'winning' because nobody questions their starting assumptions. What is the Euclidean move?","choices":["Argue louder than they do","Surface the fifth postulate: name the unexamined premise and ask what follows if it is false","Change the subject quickly","Accept all premises as given and argue the details"],"answer":1,"explain":"Euclid's lesson generalizes beyond geometry: most disputes are not about logic but about the load-bearing assumption nobody examined. Making premises explicit turns a shouting match into a checkable argument — and sometimes the 'obvious' premise is the false one, just as flat-surface geometry failed on curved spacetime."}
],
"geometry:2": [
{"q":"A 13-foot ladder leans against a wall with its foot 5 feet from the wall. How high does it reach?","choices":["12 feet","13 feet","11 feet","18 feet"],"answer":0,"explain":"The ladder is the hypotenuse: 5^2 + h^2 = 13^2, so h^2 = 169 - 25 = 144, and h = 12 feet. This is the 3-4-5 triangle scaled by 4 — spotting integer triples saves the arithmetic, and it is the same family of triples behind the rope-and-knots right angle."},
{"q":"Why is the tilted-square diagram a proof of Pythagoras, not just a picture?","choices":["Because it is drawn very precisely","Because the algebra shows it holds for any a and b — generality, not one measured example","Because Euclid himself drew it","Because pictures persuade better than words"],"answer":1,"explain":"A measured 3-4-5 triangle is evidence; the argument covers every right triangle at once: the big square's area is (a+b)^2 on one hand and 2ab + c^2 on the other, for any a and b. Proof is generality — the relationship is forced by the reasoning, not merely observed in one case."},
{"q":"A carpenter ties knots at 3, 4, and 5 unit intervals, stretches the rope into a triangle, and gets a perfect right angle. Why does this work?","choices":["Ropes naturally settle into right angles","3^2 + 4^2 = 9 + 16 = 25 = 5^2, so by the converse of Pythagoras the angle opposite the 5-side must be right","The knots are placed by magic","Any triangle with integer sides is right-angled"],"answer":1,"explain":"The theorem works in reverse too: if the sides satisfy a^2 + b^2 = c^2, the triangle must be right-angled. Egyptian surveyors and modern carpenters both exploit this to conjure perfect right angles from rope — no protractor, just arithmetic deciding the shape."}
],
"geometry:3": [
{"q":"What was Archimedes' key idea for bounding pi?","choices":["Measuring thousands of real circles","Squeezing the circle between inscribed and circumscribed polygons — more sides, tighter squeeze","Guessing 3.14 and checking it","Using a very long piece of string"],"answer":1,"explain":"A polygon inside the circle underestimates the circumference; one outside overestimates it. As the number of sides grows, both perimeters close in on the true circumference, trapping pi in an ever-narrower interval — 3.1408 to 3.1429, computed by hand over two thousand years ago. It is the squeeze idea, two millennia before calculus named it."},
{"q":"A circular garden has radius 7 m. How much fencing does it need, and what is its area?","choices":["44 m of fence and 154 m^2 of garden","22 m of fence and 49 m^2","44 m of fence and 44 m^2","154 m of fence and 44 m^2"],"answer":0,"explain":"Circumference = 2 x (22/7) x 7 = 44 m of fence. Area = (22/7) x 49 = 154 m^2. Radius 7 is the classic setup because the 7 cancels the 22/7 approximation of pi — the ancients chose friendly numbers too, and you should notice when a problem is handing you one."},
{"q":"Why is pi's constancy — the same ratio for every circle — significant?","choices":["It makes the formulas short to write","Every circle in the universe obeys the same ratio: a universal invariant discovered, not invented","It proves circles are perfect shapes","It was mainly useful for ancient tax collection"],"answer":1,"explain":"Measure any circle anywhere — a coin, a wheel, a planet's orbit — and circumference divided by diameter is always pi. A single number governing all circles is a genuine wonder: mathematics discovering a universal, not humans inventing a convention. And pi turned out to be irrational and transcendental — proven facts, not guesses."}
],
"geometry:4": [
{"q":"A 16-inch pizza versus an 8-inch pizza. How much more pizza is the 16-inch?","choices":["Twice as much","Four times as much","Eight times as much","The same — the box looks similar"],"answer":1,"explain":"Area scales with the square of the linear dimension: the diameter doubles, so the area quadruples (2^2 = 4). The 16-inch is four 8-inch pizzas. This tyranny of scale is why the big pizza is almost always the better deal — verify it with pi x r^2 and you will never unsee it."},
{"q":"A model is built at 1:10 scale. How much paint (surface) and material (volume) does it need, relative to the real thing?","choices":["1/10 the paint and 1/10 the material","1/100 the paint and 1/1000 the material","1/1000 the paint and 1/100 the material","The same — scale does not affect paint or material"],"answer":1,"explain":"Linear scale 1/10 sends area to (1/10)^2 = 1/100 and volume to (1/10)^3 = 1/1000. Paint covers surface (area); material fills space (volume). This is why models are cheap and prototypes deceive — volume collapses far faster than surface, so small versions lie about big-version costs."},
{"q":"Why can't ants be human-sized? Give the scaling argument.","choices":["They would get lost at that size","Weight grows with volume (cubed) while leg strength grows with cross-section (squared) — scaled up, the legs cannot carry the weight","Ants are afraid of heights","Their exoskeletons would dissolve in air"],"answer":1,"explain":"Double every dimension: leg cross-section (strength) quadruples, but body weight (volume) octuples. At human scale the mismatch is catastrophic — the square-cube law. Large animals need disproportionately thick bones for the same reason. Scaling is not just resizing; it is rebalancing."}
],
"geometry:5": [
{"q":"A 6-foot person casts a 4-foot shadow. At the same moment, a tree casts a 40-foot shadow. How tall is the tree?","choices":["40 feet","60 feet","26.7 feet","96 feet"],"answer":1,"explain":"Similar triangles share one ratio: person to shadow is 6:4 = 1.5, so the tree is 1.5 x 40 = 60 feet. No climbing required — similarity lets you measure the unreachable from the reachable, which is how Thales measured the Great Pyramid from its shadow."},
{"q":"Why do similar triangles guarantee the shadow method works?","choices":["Shadows are always proportional to height by nature","The sun's rays hit the ground at the same angle everywhere nearby, so both triangles share the same angles — same angles, same shape, same ratios","Trees and people are similar shapes","It only works exactly at noon"],"answer":1,"explain":"Both triangles have a right angle (object to ground) and share the sun's angle; two matching angles force the third to match, so the triangles are similar. Same shape means all corresponding lengths sit in the same ratio. The guarantee lives in the angles, not in the measuring tape."},
{"q":"A map's scale reads '1 inch = 1 mile.' Two towns are 3.5 inches apart on the map. How far apart are they, and what principle is at work?","choices":["3.5 miles — the map is a similarity transformation of the land","3.5 inches — maps cannot convey distance","35 miles","Exactly 1 mile"],"answer":0,"explain":"Every map is a similarity transformation: same shape, scaled lengths, one ratio throughout. So 3.5 inches x 1 mile/inch = 3.5 miles. Thales' pyramid trick, the map in your glove box, and perspective drawing are all the same quiet engine: same shape, different size, computable ratio."}
],
"geometry:6": [
{"q":"Eratosthenes found a 7.2-degree shadow angle at Alexandria while the sun shone straight down a well at Syene. Why did 7.2 degrees mean 'one-fiftieth of the Earth's circumference'?","choices":["7.2 x 50 = 360: the angle is 1/50 of a full circle, so the arc between the cities is 1/50 of the circumference","7.2 is a numerologically lucky number","He measured 50 different wells","His measuring stick was 50 cm long"],"answer":0,"explain":"7.2/360 = 1/50. The shadow angle equals the central angle between the two cities (parallel sun rays cut by the Earth's radii give alternate interior angles). So the Syene-Alexandria arc is 1/50 of the full circle: multiply the overland distance by 50 and you have the planet's circumference — within a few percent, from a stick and a well."},
{"q":"What is the real lesson of the capstone measurement tasks?","choices":["Ancient people were geniuses and we are not","Big things can be measured with small tools when you chain ratios and angles correctly — the math does the reaching","Real measurement requires expensive instruments","Geometry is only about shapes on paper"],"answer":1,"explain":"Thales measured a pyramid with a shadow; Eratosthenes measured the Earth with a stick. The pattern: find the ratio or angle that links the reachable to the unreachable, and let geometry do the reaching. Instruments extend the senses; theorems extend the reach."},
{"q":"In the tilted-square proof of Pythagoras, the big square's area is expressed two ways. What are they?","choices":["(a+b)^2 on one hand; four triangles (2ab) plus the inner square c^2 on the other — equating them gives a^2 + b^2 = c^2","a^2 + b^2 on one hand and c^2 on the other","4ab on one hand and (a-b)^2 on the other","It cannot be expressed two ways"],"answer":0,"explain":"The big square is (a+b)^2 = a^2 + 2ab + b^2. The same region is four right triangles (4 x ab/2 = 2ab) plus the tilted inner square c^2. Set them equal, cancel 2ab, and a^2 + b^2 = c^2 drops out. If you can rebuild this from memory, labeling every step, you do not just know the theorem — you own it."}
]
});

Object.assign(window.ACADEMY_DATA.quizzes, {
"music:1": [
{"q":"Why does the octave sound like 'the same note, higher' instead of a different note?","choices":["Because it is the same frequency","At the 2:1 ratio the upper wave completes exactly two vibrations per one of the lower, so the waves align perfectly and the ear fuses them","Because all cultures agreed to pretend it does","Because octaves are always played quietly"],"answer":1,"explain":"Every musical culture treats the octave as equivalent, and the reason is physical: at double the frequency the wave patterns lock together, peaks coinciding in perfect regularity. The ear hears one fused sound with a brighter color, not two notes — the math of vibration and the wiring of the ear agreeing across all cultures."},
{"q":"A piano and a guitar play the same note — same fundamental frequency — but sound different. Why?","choices":["They are actually playing different notes","Timbre: the different blend of harmonics (1x, 2x, 3x...) each instrument emphasizes","One of them must be out of tune","The rooms they are in are different"],"answer":1,"explain":"No real note is a single frequency — every pluck or strike sounds the harmonic series at once, with each overtone's loudness shaped by the instrument. That blend is timbre: the same fundamental dressed in different overtone clothing. Hum a low note and listen inside it — the shimmer an octave up is the second harmonic, and once you hear it you will hear overtones everywhere."},
{"q":"A string vibrates at 110 Hz. Which frequencies make up its harmonic series, and which one is the octave?","choices":["110, 220, 330, 440 Hz — the octave is 220 Hz (2x)","110, 115, 120, 125 Hz — the octave is 115 Hz","220, 440, 880 Hz — the fundamental does not count","Only 110 Hz — harmonics are purely theoretical"],"answer":0,"explain":"The harmonic series is integer multiples of the base: 1x = 110 Hz (fundamental), 2x = 220 Hz, 3x = 330 Hz, 4x = 440 Hz, growing quieter as they rise. The second harmonic, at exactly double the frequency, is the octave — the 'same note, higher' that every culture on Earth recognizes as equivalent."}
],
"music:2": [
{"q":"On a monochord, which string-length ratios produce the octave, the perfect fifth, and the perfect fourth?","choices":["Octave 2:1, fifth 3:2, fourth 4:3","Octave 3:2, fifth 2:1, fourth 4:3","Octave 4:3, fifth 3:2, fourth 2:1","All three are produced by 2:1"],"answer":0,"explain":"Halve the string (2:1) and you get the octave; take two-thirds (3:2) for the perfect fifth; three-quarters (4:3) for the perfect fourth. Shorter string, higher pitch — and the intervals every culture finds most beautiful are the simplest fractions. This was Pythagoras's staggering discovery: beauty is ratio."},
{"q":"Why do simple ratios (2:1, 3:2) sound consonant while complex ones sound dissonant?","choices":["Tradition — we were taught to hear it that way","Simple ratios realign frequently, giving regular wave patterns the ear resolves cleanly; complex ratios interfere irregularly, which the ear reads as roughness","It is only about loudness differences","Simple ratios are played on better instruments"],"answer":1,"explain":"At 3:2 the two waves' peaks coincide in a frequent, predictable pattern that the auditory system locks onto cleanly. At a complex ratio like 1.4x, realignment is rare and irregular, producing interference the ear hears as roughness or 'beats.' Try it with a tone generator: 1.5x sings, 1.4x grates — the ugliness of the complex ratio is the whole lesson."},
{"q":"A note sounds at 300 Hz. A perfect fifth above it is at what frequency?","choices":["450 Hz (300 x 3/2)","600 Hz","400 Hz","500 Hz"],"answer":0,"explain":"The perfect fifth is the 3:2 ratio: multiply the frequency by 3/2, so 300 x 1.5 = 450 Hz. On the string that is the two-thirds point — frequency ratios invert string-length ratios, since a shorter string vibrates faster. Ratios stack by multiplication, exactly like the lesson's successive percentages."}
],
"music:3": [
{"q":"The white keys from C to C sound bright; the same white keys from A to A sound dark. How?","choices":["The piano is tuned differently around A","Same notes, different home: the ear orients to the tonic, and the intervals from home differ — major third versus minor third","A is simply a lower pitch than C","White keys change their sound with the time of day"],"answer":1,"explain":"Context, not content, makes the mood. The ear hears every note relative to 'home' (the tonic). From C, home sits inside a bright major third; from A, inside a darker minor third — same raw material, different relationships, different world. That is what a mode is: a scale plus which rung you treat as home."},
{"q":"Match each mode to its character: Dorian, Phrygian, Lydian, Mixolydian.","choices":["Dorian: serious and steadfast; Phrygian: dark and exotic; Lydian: dreamy; Mixolydian: bluesy and grounded","Dorian: dreamy; Phrygian: bluesy; Lydian: serious; Mixolydian: dark","All modes sound identical in character","Modes differ only in tempo, not character"],"answer":0,"explain":"The Greeks named these characters millennia ago and they are still in use: Dorian steadfastness in folk song ('Scarborough Fair'), Phrygian darkness in flamenco, Lydian float in film scores, Mixolydian grit in the blues. Plato took mode-character so seriously he wanted some modes banned from his ideal city — an overreach, but notice the seriousness behind it."},
{"q":"Plato wanted certain modes banned from his ideal city. What does that reveal about the Greek view of music?","choices":["They disliked music","They believed music shapes character — it trains the soul, not just entertains it","They were unable to play those modes","It was really a tax policy in disguise"],"answer":1,"explain":"Banning a mode only makes sense if you believe music molds the listener's character. The Greeks filed music under mathematics and moral education alike: the mode you inhabit shapes the soul you become. Plato's overreach reveals the premise — music is formative, not decorative."}
],
"music:4": [
{"q":"Match the meter to its feel: 4/4, 3/4, 6/8.","choices":["4/4: pop, rock, and marches; 3/4: waltzes; 6/8: rolling jig feel","4/4: waltzes; 3/4: pop songs; 6/8: marches","They all feel exactly the same","Meter only matters to drummers"],"answer":0,"explain":"The time signature is a contract with the listener's body: 4/4 marches feet in fours (most pop and rock), 3/4 sways them in waltz threes, 6/8 rolls them along in jig rhythm ('We Are the Champions' verses). Clap ONE-two-three-four against ONE-two-three and feel your body want to move differently — meter tells feet when to move."},
{"q":"The Iliad is in dactylic hexameter — six feet per line, each a dactyl (LONG-short-short). Why did Greek orators care about rhythmic prose?","choices":["It filled stage time","Rhythm acts on the body and emotions before the mind finishes parsing — a well-metered phrase feels inevitable and carries the argument past the audience's defenses","Audiences legally demanded poetry","It was required by Athenian law"],"answer":1,"explain":"Rhythm bypasses analysis and goes straight to the pulse. Greek students learned meter by stamping their feet because they understood: the body hears the argument before the mind does. When a speech 'moves' you, check the meter — it is doing half the work."},
{"q":"A friend says rhythm is just 'the beat.' What is the correction?","choices":["They are right — it is the same thing","Beat is the pulse; rhythm is what you do with it; meter groups the beats — three distinct layers","Rhythm is only for professionals","Beat does not exist in classical music"],"answer":1,"explain":"Beat is the raw pulse, rhythm is the pattern played against it, and meter is how beats are grouped — the contract with the body. Conflating them is like calling bricks, walls, and floor plans 'the building.' The art lives in how the layers interact."}
],
"music:5": [
{"q":"Why does 'repetition with variation' dominate popular music form?","choices":["Songwriter laziness","Repetition makes music learnable — the brain predicts and enjoys confirming predictions; variation keeps prediction from becoming boredom","It is cheaper to record","Radio regulations require it"],"answer":1,"explain":"The brain is a prediction machine and rewards confirmed predictions with pleasure — that is the hook of the chorus. But pure repetition becomes wallpaper, so the bridge departs and the verse varies: just enough surprise to keep the predictor engaged. The balance of the two is the whole game of musical pleasure."},
{"q":"What is each pass for in the three-pass active listening practice?","choices":["Each pass is simply louder than the last","First: the whole and the feeling; second: follow one layer only; third: map the form and spot what changed on each return","First: read the lyrics; second: rest; third: assign a rating","It does not matter — any three random listens work"],"answer":1,"explain":"Pass one surrenders to the whole; pass two isolates a single thread (try the bass line — it reveals the harmony's skeleton); pass three maps the architecture: where each section starts and what is different this time through. Three focused passes teach more than a hundred background plays — attention is the instrument."},
{"q":"In the verse-chorus-bridge skeleton, what job does each section do?","choices":["They are interchangeable","The verse tells, the chorus declares, the bridge departs — then home again","The verse is loud, the chorus is quiet, the bridge is silent","Only the chorus matters"],"answer":1,"explain":"The verse advances the story, the chorus states the thesis (and invites singing along), the bridge breaks the pattern to create tension before the final return. Once you hear the skeleton, you stop being washed along and start following — noticing what changed this time through, which is where the artistry lives."}
],
"music:6": [
{"q":"Your capstone analysis finds maximum tension just before the final chorus, released when the chorus returns in a higher key. What are you actually describing?","choices":["The loudness curve","Form doing its job: the bridge's departure creates tension, and the return home — repetition with variation — releases it","Bad mixing by the engineer","A mistake the band made"],"answer":1,"explain":"Tension and release is form made audible. The bridge departs from the established pattern (tension); the chorus's return restores the familiar with a twist (release). Mapping where tension peaks and how it is released is the core skill of structural listening — you are hearing the architecture, not just the decoration."},
{"q":"You compose an 8-bar body-percussion pattern with a clear meter, one repeating motif, and one variation. Which quadrivium idea are you embodying?","choices":["None — composing is not mathematics","Music as number made audible: meter is counting, motifs are ratios of time, form is architecture — the math inside the beauty","Only professionals can embody Greek ideas","Body percussion is not real music"],"answer":1,"explain":"Your 8 bars are arithmetic (counting beats), geometry (the shape of the phrase), and ratio (motif against variation) — the quadrivium in your chest and hands. The Greeks filed music with mathematics because harmony is number made audible; after this course you should be able to hear the numbers inside the beauty."},
{"q":"Playback of your recorded rhythm reveals your 'variation' actually breaks the meter. What is the lesson?","choices":["Recording yourself is useless","The playback never lies: variation must stay inside the contract with the listener's body — break the meter and you break the groove","Meter does not matter","Delete the recording immediately"],"answer":1,"explain":"The capstone warns: record it and listen back, because the playback never lies. A variation that breaks the meter is not daring — it is a broken contract with the listener's body, which was counting on the pattern. True variation plays against the meter, not through it."}
]
});

Object.assign(window.ACADEMY_DATA.flashcards, {
"arithmetic": [
{"front":"Fermi problem","back":"An estimation question answered by breaking the unknown into guessable pieces and multiplying them — e.g., piano tuners in Chicago from households x pianos x tunings. Aim: within a factor of two. Hook: 'Fermi' = factor-two physics."},
{"front":"Order of magnitude","back":"A power of ten. A million seconds is ~11 days; a billion is ~32 years — 'both big' hides a 1,000x gap. Habit: convert every big number to a scale your brain can feel."},
{"front":"'Percent of what?'","back":"The defense against percentage deception: every % needs its base. 'Unemployment fell 20%' — from 10% to 8%, or from 5% to 4%? Also demand the absolute change and the timeframe."},
{"front":"Successive percent changes","back":"They MULTIPLY, never add. $100 -> 50% off -> $50 -> 50% up -> $75, not $100. Order does not matter; multiplication is multiplication — the merchant's favorite asymmetry."},
{"front":"The 10% trick","back":"Move the decimal one place: 10% of anything is instant. Build 5% (half of 10%), 15% (10+5), 20% (double 10%) from it. Tip on $47.60: $4.76 + $2.38 = $7.14."},
{"front":"Proof by contradiction","back":"Assume the opposite, build from that assumption, watch it explode. Euclid: assume finitely many primes -> N = (their product) + 1 needs a new prime divisor -> contradiction. Settles questions forever, not just for now."},
{"front":"Mean vs. median","back":"The mean is dragged by outliers: nine $30k earners + one $3M earner = $327k mean, $30k median. For money, always ask WHICH average — the median describes the typical case."},
{"front":"Base rate","back":"How common a condition is before testing. A '99% accurate' test for a 1-in-10,000 disease yields ~100 false positives per true one. Accuracy without the base rate is a story without a setting."}
],
"geometry": [
{"front":"Euclid's five postulates","back":"1) A line between any two points. 2) Segments extend indefinitely. 3) A circle from any center and radius. 4) All right angles are equal. 5) The parallel postulate — the itchy one. Explicit foundations make reasoning checkable."},
{"front":"Parallel postulate","back":"Through a point not on a line, exactly one parallel line can be drawn. Two millennia of failed proof attempts led to non-Euclidean geometry — which describes curved spacetime. Hook: disputes are usually about the unexamined fifth postulate."},
{"front":"Pythagoras' theorem","back":"a^2 + b^2 = c^2 for right triangles. Visual proof: a square of side (a+b) equals four triangles (2ab) plus the tilted inner square (c^2). Generality is what makes it proof, not picture."},
{"front":"3-4-5 triangle","back":"3^2 + 4^2 = 9 + 16 = 25 = 5^2. Knot a rope at 3, 4, 5 intervals and stretch it into a triangle for a perfect right angle (the converse of Pythagoras). Egyptian surveyors' trick, still used by carpenters."},
{"front":"Pi","back":"Circumference / diameter — constant for every circle, ~3.14159. Archimedes squeezed it between polygons: 3.1408 to 3.1429, by hand. Irrational and transcendental — proven, not guessed."},
{"front":"Circle area intuition","back":"Slice a circle into thin wedges and rearrange them into a near-rectangle: height r, width half the circumference (pi x r) -> area = pi x r^2. Formulas you build, you keep."},
{"front":"Square-cube law","back":"Double the edge -> area x4 (2^2), volume x8 (2^3). A 16-inch pizza is FOUR 8-inch pizzas. Ants cannot be human-sized: cubed weight overwhelms squared leg strength."},
{"front":"Similarity (Thales' shadow)","back":"Same shape, different size — all lengths in one ratio. The sun's rays give matching angles, so person:shadow = tree:shadow. 6 ft / 4 ft shadow -> a 40 ft shadow means a 60 ft tree. Measure the unreachable."}
],
"music": [
{"front":"Harmonic series","back":"A vibrating string sounds 1x, 2x, 3x, 4x the base frequency all at once, getting quieter. The blend of overtones = TIMBRE — why a piano and a guitar differ on the same note."},
{"front":"Octave (2:1)","back":"Double the frequency (halve the string) -> 'the same note, higher.' Every culture hears it as equivalent: the 2:1 waves lock perfectly and the ear fuses them into one brighter sound."},
{"front":"Perfect fifth (3:2) and fourth (4:3)","back":"Two-thirds of the string (3:2) = the fifth; three-quarters (4:3) = the fourth. Beauty is ratio: the simplest fractions sound most consonant because their waves realign most often."},
{"front":"Consonance vs. dissonance","back":"Simple ratios realign frequently -> clean patterns the ear resolves (consonant). Complex ratios interfere irregularly -> roughness or 'beats' (dissonant). Demo: 1.5x a tone sings; 1.4x grates."},
{"front":"Mode","back":"A scale plus which rung is home. Same white keys: C->C is bright major, A->A is dark minor. Context, not content, makes the mood. Dorian = steadfast, Phrygian = dark, Lydian = dreamy, Mixolydian = bluesy."},
{"front":"Meter: 4/4, 3/4, 6/8","back":"How beats are grouped — a contract with the listener's body. 4/4: pop, rock, marches. 3/4: waltzes. 6/8: jigs. The Greeks scanned poetry the same way (dactylic hexameter: LONG-short-short)."},
{"front":"Form: repetition with variation","back":"Verse tells, chorus declares, bridge departs — then home again. Repetition lets the brain predict (pleasure); variation prevents boredom. The balance of the two is the whole game of musical pleasure."},
{"front":"Three-pass listening","back":"1) The whole and the feeling. 2) Follow ONE layer only (bass line, drums). 3) Map the form — where sections start, what changed on each return. Three focused passes beat a hundred background plays."}
]
});

Object.assign(window.ACADEMY_DATA.exams, {
"arithmetic": [
{"q":"Fermi estimate: a city has 320,000 households, 1 in 20 owns a piano, each piano is tuned once a year, and a tuner completes 500 tunings a year. About how many piano tuners does the city support?","choices":["32","320","3","3,200"],"answer":0,"explain":"320,000 / 20 = 16,000 pianos; one tuning each per year = 16,000 tunings; 16,000 / 500 = 32 tuners. The pieces are guesses, but they are independent — errors partly cancel, landing within a factor of two. That is the Fermi promise: no data, right neighborhood."},
{"q":"A U.S. dollar bill is about 0.11 mm thick. How tall is a stack of a billion $1 bills?","choices":["About 110 km — past airliner cruising altitude","About 1.1 km","About 11 km","About 1,100 km"],"answer":0,"explain":"10^9 x 0.11 mm = 110,000 m = 110 km, up toward the edge of space. A million bills stack to just 110 m — a football field stood on end. Same 'big number,' utterly different universes: orders of magnitude made physical."},
{"q":"A $240 jacket is 25% off, then an extra 10% off the sale price. What is the final price?","choices":["$162","$156 (35% off)","$180","$144"],"answer":0,"explain":"Multiply, don't add: $240 x 0.75 = $180, then $180 x 0.90 = $162. Adding the percentages (35% off = $156) is the merchant's favorite error — the second discount applies to the smaller sale price, so it is worth less. Always run the multiplication."},
{"q":"Paper towels: a 24-pack for $8.99 versus a 12-pack for $4.79. Which is the better buy?","choices":["The 24-pack: ~$0.375 each beats ~$0.399 each","The 12-pack — the total price is lower","They are exactly equal","Impossible to tell without the brand"],"answer":0,"explain":"$8.99 / 24 is about $0.375 per roll; $4.79 / 12 is about $0.399. The bulk pack wins by ~2.4 cents a roll — small per item, real across a year of them. Unit pricing defeats the 'big deal' illusion in two seconds."},
{"q":"What is 15% of $86, computed mentally?","choices":["$12.90","$11.40","$13.50","$12.60"],"answer":0,"explain":"Anchor on 10%: move the decimal — $8.60. Half of that is 5%: $4.30. Add: $8.60 + $4.30 = $12.90. The decimal-shift plus halving handles every common percentage with no calculator, and the estimate in your head is the audit on every bill."},
{"q":"Someone claims 'there are exactly 1,000 prime numbers.' What does Euclid's construction do to this claim?","choices":["Builds N = (product of all 1,000) + 1, which needs a prime divisor outside the list — the list can never be complete","Produces a bigger list of 1,001 primes","Proves that 1,000 is prime","Nothing — the proof only works for small lists"],"answer":0,"explain":"Multiply the claimed complete list and add 1. Every listed prime leaves remainder 1 dividing N, yet N must have some prime divisor — a new prime, contradiction. The argument does not care whether the list has 10 primes or 1,000: any finite list self-destructs. That is the power of generality."},
{"q":"A company has 10 employees: the CEO earns $2M, nine workers earn $50k each. A recruiting ad boasts an 'average salary of $245k.' What is wrong?","choices":["Mean $245k, median $50k — the ad describes nobody typical","Mean $50k, median $245k","Both the mean and median are $245k","The median is $2M"],"answer":0,"explain":"Mean = $2,450,000 / 10 = $245k; median = $50k, the middle value of nine $50k salaries and one $2M. The ad is technically true and practically a lie — nine of ten employees earn less than a quarter of the quoted figure. Which average? Always ask."},
{"q":"A disease affects 1 in 5,000 people. A test is 98% accurate (2% false positives). In 100,000 people tested, roughly how do true and false positives compare?","choices":["~20 true positives vs ~2,000 false positives — about 99% of positives are false alarms","98% of positives are true cases","True and false positives are about equal","False positives are impossible at 98% accuracy"],"answer":0,"explain":"About 20 sick people test positive, but 2% of ~100,000 healthy people — roughly 2,000 — also test positive. Of ~2,020 positives, only ~20 are real: ~99% false alarms. '98% accurate' sounds reassuring until the base rate does the arithmetic — which is why screening for rare conditions demands follow-up testing."},
{"q":"A post brags 'markets up 60% since March!' What is the first thing to check?","choices":["What the market did BEFORE March — a cherry-picked start date can manufacture any trend; demand the longer series","Whether 60% counts as a big number","The name of the poster's broker","Nothing — 60% is 60%"],"answer":0,"explain":"Start the clock at a dip and any recovery looks like a boom. Run the teardown: compared to what, measured how, over which period, who benefits? Here 'over which period' is decisive — show the five-year chart, not the flattering slice."},
{"q":"A viral post claims 'the average American will spend $1 trillion in their lifetime.' What two moves kill it?","choices":["Fermi-check the scale (~$2M lifetime earnings makes $1T impossible — off by ~500,000x), then ask who benefits from the viral share","Share it — big numbers are impressive","Search for the exact phrase to verify it","Assume it must be about billionaires"],"answer":0,"explain":"Move one, Fermi-scale it: ~$50k/year x 40 years is about $2M in lifetime earnings, so $1T in spending is off by roughly half a million times — impossible on its face. Move two, the fourth master question: who benefits from me believing and sharing it? Virality rewards the outrageous, not the true. Scale first, motives second — most viral numbers die at step one."}
],
"geometry": [
{"q":"On the Earth's surface, 'lines' are great circles — and any two great circles intersect. What does this say about Euclid's fifth postulate?","choices":["Euclid was wrong about everything","The fifth postulate is independent: curved surfaces need different rules — which is why non-Euclidean geometry describes curved spacetime","Spheres contain no lines at all","Parallel lines are impossible everywhere"],"answer":1,"explain":"Great-circle 'lines' on a sphere have no parallels — yet the geometry is perfectly consistent. This does not refute Euclid; it shows the fifth postulate is a choice, not a logical necessity. Change the assumption and you get a new geometry — and Einstein needed exactly that geometry for general relativity. Question the fifth postulate."},
{"q":"A rectangular garden is 9 m by 12 m. How long is the diagonal path across it?","choices":["15 m","21 m","10.5 m","18 m"],"answer":0,"explain":"9^2 + 12^2 = 81 + 144 = 225 = 15^2, so the diagonal is 15 m — the 3-4-5 triangle scaled by 3. Spotting the scaled triple beats grinding square roots, and it is the same family of triples behind the rope-and-knots right angle."},
{"q":"A triangle has sides 7, 24, and 25. Is it right-angled?","choices":["Yes: 7^2 + 24^2 = 49 + 576 = 625 = 25^2, so the angle opposite the 25-side is right","No","Only a protractor can decide","Impossible to tell without the angles"],"answer":0,"explain":"The theorem runs both ways: sides satisfying a^2 + b^2 = c^2 force a right angle opposite the longest side. 49 + 576 = 625 exactly — no measurement needed; the arithmetic decides. Integer triples like 7-24-25 are the surveyor's free right angles."},
{"q":"A bicycle wheel has diameter 70 cm. How far does it travel in one revolution — and in 100?","choices":["2.2 m per revolution; 220 m per 100","0.7 m per revolution; 70 m per 100","1.4 m per revolution; 140 m per 100","3.5 m per revolution; 350 m per 100"],"answer":0,"explain":"One revolution lays the circumference on the ground: pi x 70 cm is about 220 cm = 2.2 m. A hundred revolutions: 220 m. Circumference = pi x d is the odometer's founding equation — every mile your car logs is this multiplication, repeated."},
{"q":"Why do more polygon sides give Archimedes a better bound on pi?","choices":["Bigger numbers are always better","More sides make the polygons hug the circle more tightly, so inner and outer perimeters converge on the true circumference — the trap narrows","Archimedes simply liked polygons","The circle itself gets bigger"],"answer":1,"explain":"Each added side straightens the polygon's approximation of the curve. The inscribed perimeter rises toward the circumference from below; the circumscribed falls toward it from above — the squeeze tightens with every side. It is the limit idea, two thousand years before calculus gave it a name."},
{"q":"A 3D-printer scales a model up 3x in every dimension. How do paint (surface) and plastic (volume) needs change?","choices":["9x the paint, 27x the plastic","3x for both","27x the paint, 9x the plastic","9x for both"],"answer":0,"explain":"Linear x3 sends area to 3^2 = 9 (paint covers surface) and volume to 3^3 = 27 (plastic fills space). Scaling punishes volume far more than surface — the same tyranny that makes a 16-inch pizza four 8-inch pizzas and keeps ants small."},
{"q":"Why do elephants need proportionally thicker legs than gazelles?","choices":["For running faster","Weight scales with volume (cubed) but bone strength with cross-section (squared) — bigger animals need disproportionately thicker supports","Thick legs look impressive","They do not — it is random"],"answer":1,"explain":"Scale an animal up 2x: weight x8, leg cross-section x4 — the legs are suddenly half as strong relative to the load. Evolution compensates with disproportionately thick bones. Galileo spotted this in 1638; it is also why building-sized movie monsters would collapse under their own weight."},
{"q":"Your 1.8 m friend casts a 1.2 m shadow; a flagpole's shadow is 9 m. How tall is the flagpole?","choices":["13.5 m","9 m","6 m","10.8 m"],"answer":0,"explain":"Same sun angle means similar triangles and one shared ratio: 1.8/1.2 = 1.5, so the pole is 1.5 x 9 = 13.5 m. Thales' pyramid trick in modern dress — the reachable measuring the unreachable through a single ratio."},
{"q":"Eratosthenes: Syene and Alexandria were 800 km apart — 1/50 of the circumference. What are Earth's circumference and radius?","choices":["Circumference 40,000 km; radius about 6,366 km","Circumference 800 km","Radius 40,000 km","Circumference 25,000 km"],"answer":0,"explain":"800 km x 50 = 40,000 km around — within a few percent of the true 40,075 km. Radius = 40,000 / (2 x pi), about 6,366 km against the true 6,371. A stick, a well, and similar triangles measured the planet: the capstone's whole moral in a single calculation."},
{"q":"Someone 'proves' all triangles are right-angled by measuring 50 examples. What is wrong with this?","choices":["Nothing — 50 examples is plenty","Measurement is evidence, not proof: it cannot cover all triangles and carries instrument error — proof needs a general argument","Triangles are never right-angled","50 is too few; 500 examples would prove it"],"answer":1,"explain":"Fifty measurements — even a million — cover only the measured cases and inherit instrument error. Proof demands a reason it must hold for every case. That is why the visual proof's algebra matters: it speaks about all a and b at once, where examples speak only about themselves."}
],
"music": [
{"q":"A string sounds A at 220 Hz. What is the octave above it, and a perfect fifth above that octave?","choices":["Octave 440 Hz; fifth above it 660 Hz","Octave 440 Hz; fifth above it 550 Hz","Octave 330 Hz; fifth above it 495 Hz","The octave is still 220 Hz"],"answer":0,"explain":"Octave = 2x: 220 -> 440 Hz. A fifth above any note = 3/2x: 440 x 1.5 = 660 Hz — the third harmonic of the original string, which is why the fifth rings so sympathetically against it. Ratios stack by multiplication, exactly like successive percentages."},
{"q":"A 90 cm string sounds a note. Where do you stop it to get the perfect fifth? The octave?","choices":["Fifth at 60 cm, octave at 45 cm","Fifth at 45 cm, octave at 60 cm","Fifth at 30 cm, octave at 90 cm","Both at 45 cm"],"answer":0,"explain":"Shorter string, higher pitch — frequency ratios invert string-length ratios. Fifth (3:2 frequency) = 2/3 of the length = 60 cm; octave (2:1) = 1/2 = 45 cm. Pythagoras's monochord: the beautiful intervals are the simple fractions, in string and in sound alike."},
{"q":"Two flutes play A440; one sounds 'breathy,' the other 'pure.' Same pitch — what differs?","choices":["The frequency","The harmonic blend: the breathy flute strengthens higher overtones; the pure one is closer to the fundamental alone","Only the volume","One of them is out of tune"],"answer":1,"explain":"Same fundamental, different overtone recipe. The 'pure' tone is nearly a lone sine wave; the 'breathy' one carries audible higher harmonics. Timbre is the harmonic series' fingerprint — why a piano and a guitar differ on the same note, and why two flutes can too."},
{"q":"Which pair is more consonant — 200+300 Hz or 200+280 Hz — and why?","choices":["200+300 Hz: the 2:3 ratio realigns frequently for clean resolution, while 200:280 (5:7) interferes irregularly","200+280 Hz: closer pitches always blend better","They are equally consonant","Neither — consonance is a myth"],"answer":0,"explain":"200:300 reduces to 2:3 — the perfect fifth, waves realigning every few cycles. 200:280 reduces to 5:7, realigning rarely and producing the roughness the ear reads as dissonance. It is not taste; it is arithmetic the auditory system performs on every pair of tones."},
{"q":"A folk tune uses only the white keys but 'homes' on D. Which mode is this, and what is its character?","choices":["Dorian — serious and steadfast, the 'Scarborough Fair' sound","Phrygian — dark and exotic","Lydian — dreamy and floating","C major — bright and resolved"],"answer":0,"explain":"White keys from D to D form the Dorian mode. Same notes as C major, but home on D recolors every interval — the tune sounds ancient and steadfast rather than bright. Mode = scale + home: context, not content, makes the mood."},
{"q":"A piece alternates bars of 3/4 and 4/4. How many beats per two-bar phrase, and what does the listener's body feel?","choices":["7 beats per pair — the contract with the body keeps changing, creating restlessness and forward pull","6 beats — it feels like a waltz","8 beats — it feels like pop","Beats do not affect the listener"],"answer":0,"explain":"3 + 4 = 7 beats before the pattern repeats, so the listener's body never gets a stable contract — composers use this for unease or drive, and much progressive rock lives here. Meter is a promise to the feet; breaking it on purpose is a compositional tool, breaking it by accident is losing the groove."},
{"q":"Scan 'MUR-mur-ing': what foot is it, and why did Greek orators care about such feet?","choices":["A dactyl (LONG-short-short) — orators used such feet because rhythm moves the body before the mind finishes parsing, carrying arguments past defenses","An iamb — orators avoided rhythm","It has no meter","A spondee"],"answer":0,"explain":"MUR-mur-ing is LONG-short-short: a dactyl, the foot of Homer's hexameter. Greek orators stamped such rhythms into prose deliberately — the body hears the argument before the mind does, and a well-metered phrase feels inevitable. When a speech moves you, check the meter: it is doing half the work."},
{"q":"A song runs verse-chorus-verse-chorus-bridge-chorus, and the final chorus adds a choir. Analyze the variation.","choices":["Repetition with variation: the returning chorus confirms predictions (pleasure) while the choir varies it (anti-boredom) — maximum familiarity plus fresh information","The songwriter ran out of ideas","Choirs are always a mistake","Form does not affect listeners"],"answer":0,"explain":"The final chorus is the most repeated material — maximum predictability, maximum sing-along pleasure — and the choir is the variation that keeps the predictor engaged. Repetition with variation is the whole game: learnable enough to love, surprising enough to keep. Mapping such returns is the third listening pass."},
{"q":"On your second pass through a song in the three-pass practice, what do you do — and why?","choices":["Follow one layer only — e.g., the bass line — because isolating a thread reveals the harmony's skeleton hidden in the full mix","Turn it up louder","Skip to the chorus","Read the lyrics"],"answer":1,"explain":"Pass two isolates: one layer, full attention. The bass line usually traces the chord roots — follow it and the harmony's skeleton emerges from the mix. Three focused passes (whole, then layer, then form) teach more than a hundred background plays; attention is the instrument."},
{"q":"Why did the Greeks file music with arithmetic and geometry rather than with poetry?","choices":["They disliked poetry","Because harmony is number made audible — intervals are ratios, rhythm is counting, form is architecture: music is mathematics the ear can hear","It was a filing error","Poetry had not been invented yet"],"answer":1,"explain":"Octaves are 2:1, fifths 3:2, meter is grouped counting, and musical form is built like a proof — repetition with variation as logical structure. The quadrivium united them because they are one study: number in the abstract (arithmetic), number in space (geometry), number in time and vibration (music). Hear the numbers inside the beauty and the filing makes perfect sense."}
]
});
